WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.625
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:03.140
JOE KAVA: I love building
in operating data centers.

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:06.230
I'm Joe Kava, Vice President
of Data Centers at Google.

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:09.830
I'm responsible for managing
the teams globally that design,

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:12.145
build, and operate
Google's data centers.

00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:14.270
We're also responsible for
the environmental health

00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:18.510
and safety, sustainability,
and carbon offsets for our data

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:19.870
centers.

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:22.320
This data center here
in South Carolina

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:25.940
is one node in a larger
network of data centers

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:27.120
all over the world.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.660
Of all the employees at Google,
a very, very small percentage

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.870
of those employees are
authorized to even enter

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:34.460
a data center campus.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.290
I think the Google
data centers really

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:39.530
can offer a level of security
that almost no other company

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:40.330
can match.

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:43.840
We have a information
security team

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.310
that is truly second to none.

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:47.780
You have the expression,
they wrote the book on that.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:50.500
Well, there are many of
our information security

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:52.700
team members that
have really written

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:56.670
the books on best practices
in information security.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.920
Protecting the security
and the privacy

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:03.820
of our users' information is
our foremost design criteria.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:06.620
We use various layers
of higher-level security

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:09.020
the closer into the center
of the campus you get.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:11.080
So just to enter
this campus, my badge

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:13.800
had to be on a
pre-authorized access list.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:15.350
Then, to come into
the building, that

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:17.170
was another level of security.

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:20.230
To get into the secure corridor
which leads to the data center,

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:22.290
that's a higher
level of security.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.310
And the data center and
the networking rooms

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:26.160
are the highest
level of security.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.790
All of our racks don't really
look like a traditional server

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:30.450
rack.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.610
These are custom designed
and built for Google

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:35.770
so that we can
optimize the servers

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:40.820
for hyper-efficiency and
high-performance computing.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:44.360
To me, the thing that amazes
me about Google and the data

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:46.990
centers is the
pace of innovation

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:50.520
and always challenging the
way we're doing things.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:53.970
So when people say that
innovation in a certain area

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:56.710
is done, that we've, kind of,
reached the pinnacle of what

00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:59.210
can be achieved, I just laugh.

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:02.560
[MUSIC PLAYING]

